# check_coverage.py allow-list -- intentional coverage gaps only.
# Format: <os> <tag|*> <workflow>   # reason
# Every line below is backed by real evidence (a workflow comment, the
# builder's releases.json sync field, table.md/README, or git history);
# the evidence is cited in the comment block above each entry. Genuine
# coverage holes are NOT listed here; they stay failing and get reported.

# blissos (Android guest): scp is the only sync backend -- blissos.yml's
# own comment says "no rsync/sshfs/nfs on Android" (VM_SYNC_METHODS=scp in
# blissos-builder/releases.json). blissos.yml already exercises scp
# directly on ubuntu-24.04, windows-latest AND macos-latest, so the
# shared cross-host jobs below would be pure duplication.
blissos * test.yml
blissos * testmacos.yml
blissos * testwindows.yml

# dragonflybsd: testsync exercises sshfs+scp+rsync together. dragonflybsd-
# builder's table.md documents "sshfs is not offered on DragonFlyBSD --
# the sshfs (FUSE) mount is read-only in practice ... so only rsync/scp/nfs
# are listed" (releases.json sync=rsync,scp,nfs, no sshfs). dragonflybsd
# stays in testnfs/testsysnfs (nfs-only), only testsync is affected.
dragonflybsd * test.yml

# haiku: haiku.yml deliberately pins no release -- its matrix is
# release: [""], which makes anyvm fall through to find_latest_release
# over the pinned builder release's assets (anyvm.py, "if not
# config['release']"), so the job always tests whatever image
# haiku-builder's pinned version ships. For v2.0.3 that is r1beta6, the
# only releases.json entry with "build": true.
#
# r1beta5 keeps "build": false: its conf stays so the already-published
# v2.0.2 image remains documented and usable, but it is not rebuilt and
# its image does not appear under the new tag -- checked 2026-08-27,
# v2.0.2 carries haiku-r1beta5.qcow2.zst and v2.0.3 does not. Naming it
# in this matrix would point at an asset the pinned version lacks.
#
# The checker cannot resolve an empty release, so both tags read as
# uncovered even though the newest one is tested on every run.
haiku * haiku.yml

# nextbsd: test.yml's own comment above the testnfs job says it plainly --
# "nextbsd is in this job (the bundled user-space nfsd) but deliberately
# NOT in testsysnfs below: only the user-space path has been verified on
# it." nextbsd is already present in testsync/testnfs; only testsysnfs is
# affected.
nextbsd * test.yml

# omnios: omnios.yml's own comment -- "OmniOS has no FUSE sshfs
# (VM_SSHFS_PKG is empty), so sshfs is not offered" (releases.json
# sync=rsync,scp,nfs for every release). omnios stays in testnfs/
# testsysnfs; only testsync (which needs sshfs) is affected.
omnios * test.yml

# openindiana: testsync tests exactly sshfs+scp+rsync. openindiana's own
# dedicated openindiana.yml ALREADY runs that same trio (plus nfs) directly
# for every release (matrix sync: [sshfs, scp, nfs, rsync]), so duplicating
# it in the shared testsync job adds no coverage. openindiana stays in
# testnfs/testsysnfs.
openindiana * test.yml

# plan9 (9front): has no sshd, so it cannot reuse the ssh-based
# testrun.yml that testsync/testnfs/testsysnfs (and the macos/windows
# jobs) all route through -- test.yml/testmacos.yml/testwindows.yml each
# say so directly in the comment above their own dedicated "plan9" job,
# which already verifies boot + telnet-exec + 9P-sync (Linux) or telnet-
# exec (macOS/Windows) on that same workflow file.
plan9 * test.yml
plan9 * testmacos.yml
plan9 * testwindows.yml

# reactos: ReactOS ships no remote-access server at all, so reactos-builder
# bakes its own Win32 telnet server into the image; with no sshd it cannot
# reuse the ssh-based testrun.yml that testsync/testnfs/testsysnfs (and the
# macos/windows jobs) all route through, and its only sync backend is tar
# (releases.json sync=tar). test.yml, testmacos.yml and testwindows.yml each
# carry a dedicated "reactos" job instead, verifying boot + telnet exec + tar
# sync on that host -- see the comment above each of those jobs.
#
# (History worth keeping: those legs were briefly deleted as "impossible"
# after they timed out. The cause was ours, not the host's -- anyvm passed
# -rtc driftfix=slew, which breaks ReactOS' HAL stall calibration on any
# host without full hardware virtualisation. With that dropped the guest
# boots in 68 s under pure TCG and 35 s under WHPX.)
reactos * test.yml
reactos * testmacos.yml
reactos * testwindows.yml

# riscos: two independent reasons, and neither is a hole worth filling.
#
# (1) No sshd. RISC OS ships no remote-access server of ANY kind -- its whole
# networking stack is clients plus LanManFS and ShareFS/Freeway -- so
# riscos-builder bakes in its own Python agent and anyvm drives it over
# telnet. With no sshd it cannot reuse the ssh-based testrun.yml that
# testsync/testnfs/testsysnfs all route through, and its only sync backend is
# tar (releases.json sync=tar). test.yml carries a dedicated "Test riscos tar"
# job instead (the comment above it says so), which verifies boot + telnet
# exec + tar sync on the Linux host.
#
# (2) Linux x86_64 only, so the macOS and Windows jobs are not skipped
# coverage -- the guest cannot run there at all. anyvm.py says it in its own
# words: "RISC OS is Linux x86_64 only ... no released QEMU can boot RISC OS
# on a raspi machine, so the guest depends on the patched build that
# ensure_pinned_qemu() downloads -- and that only exists for Linux x86_64."
# The pinned asset is literally named for it: qemu-10.2.3-riscos-arm-noble.
riscos * test.yml
riscos * testmacos.yml
riscos * testwindows.yml

# redox: Redox 0.9.0 ships no remote-access server of any kind -- no sshd, no
# telnetd, no ftpd -- so redox-builder injects its own no_std agent into the
# image offline and anyvm drives it over telnet. With no sshd it cannot reuse
# the ssh-based testrun.yml that testsync/testnfs/testsysnfs (and the
# macos/windows jobs) all route through, and its only sync backend is tar
# (releases.json sync=tar). test.yml carries a dedicated "Test redox tar" job
# instead, and redox.yml repeats it standalone with an explicit release/arch.
#
# Unlike riscos this guest is NOT host-restricted: it is a plain x86_64 guest
# on stock QEMU, so macOS and Windows hosts should work the way they do for
# reactos. They are exempt here only because that has not been RUN yet --
# every verification so far (builder CI, and a local anyvm.py boot + exec +
# tar push against the published v2.0.0 image) was on a Linux x86_64 host.
# This is a deferred gap, not a determination: the fix is dedicated redox jobs
# in testmacos.yml and testwindows.yml mirroring the reactos ones, and these
# two lines should be deleted the day those land (maintainer note 2026-08-12).
redox * test.yml
redox * testmacos.yml
redox * testwindows.yml

# solaris: solaris.yml's own comment -- "Solaris has no FUSE sshfs
# (VM_SSHFS_PKG is unset), so sshfs is omitted" (releases.json
# sync=rsync,scp,nfs). solaris stays in testnfs/testsysnfs; only testsync
# (which needs sshfs) is affected.
solaris * test.yml

# tribblix: releases.json sync=rsync,scp,nfs for every release (no sshfs),
# and tribblix.yml's own matrix never tests sshfs either. tribblix stays
# in testnfs/testsysnfs; only testsync is affected.
tribblix * test.yml

# The -build variants (base OS plus a preinstalled build-essential
# toolchain) are deliberately untested -- maintainer decision 2026-07-26:
# the base release of each already exercises the same boot/sync paths.
omnios r151054-build omnios.yml
omnios r151056-build omnios.yml
omnios r151058-build omnios.yml
openindiana 202510-build openindiana.yml
openindiana 202604-build openindiana.yml

# The solaris toolchain variants (base 11.4 plus a specific newer
# compiler toolchain) are deliberately untested -- maintainer decision
# 2026-07-26: 11.4 and 11.4-gcc already exercise the same boot/sync paths.
solaris 11.4-gcc-14 solaris.yml
solaris 11.4-clang-19 solaris.yml
